{"maxId":273,"entries":{"chapter-76-study-heart-failure|Chapter 76 Study: Heart Failure":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"definition-amp-epidemiology|Definition & Epidemiology":["u3"],"copy|HF is a syndrome (not a single disease) resulting from any structural/functional cardiac disorder impairing ventricular filling or ejection (p1165).":["u4"],"copy|~6.5 million Americans affected; projected to exceed 8 million by 2030; >1 million new cases/year. Prevalence doubles every decade after 40, exceeding 10% in both men and women over 80 (p1166).":["u5"],"copy|Rising HF incidence/prevalence reflects more than population aging: improved survival from CAD, stroke, renal disease, cancer, and infections, plus growing obesity/diabetes, expand the pool of patients who live long enough to develop HF (Table 76-1, p1166).":["u6"],"copy|1 million primary-HF hospital admissions in 2014: 71% in patients >65y, 53% ≥75y, 25% occurred in the 2% of the population ≥85y. Almost 2 million ambulatory visits in 2016 — HF ranks 2nd only to hypertension among CV outpatient reasons (p1166).":["u7"],"copy|Annual cost >$35 billion; projected $69.8 billion by 2030 (≈$244/US adult) (p1166).":["u8"],"aging-physiology-4-principal-effects|Aging Physiology (4 principal effects)":["u9"],"copy|(1) Reduced β-adrenergic responsiveness — receptor desensitization, not reduced receptor density (p1167).":["u10"],"copy|(2) Increased arterial stiffness → increased afterload/impedance to LV ejection (p1168).":["u11"],"copy|(3) Altered diastolic filling — impaired early relaxation, increased reliance on atrial \"kick\": up to 30–40% of LV end-diastolic volume from atrial contraction in older adults (vs a small fraction in youth) (p1168–1169).":["u12"],"copy|(4) Altered mitochondrial energy metabolism — normal at rest, can't meet demand under stress (p1170).":["u13"],"copy|Restrictive Doppler filling pattern (A-wave <50% of E-wave amplitude) = advanced disease with poor prognosis, not attributable to aging alone (p1169).":["u14"],"etiology-amp-precipitants|Etiology & Precipitants":["u15"],"copy|HTN + CAD account for >70% of HF causes (p1170).":["u16"],"copy|ATTRwt (wild-type transthyretin) cardiac amyloidosis found in 13% of older adults hospitalized for HFPEF with LV wall thickness >12 mm; the ATTRv variant is present in up to 4% of African-Americans (p1171).":["u17"],"copy|High-output failure causes: chronic anemia, hyperthyroidism, thiamine deficiency, AV shunting (dialysis fistula/AVM), morbid obesity — often overlooked (p1172).":["u18"],"copy|Most common precipitant: medication/diet nonadherence, up to two-thirds of exacerbations (p1172).":["u19"],"copy|30–50% of older HF adults take ≥1 medication that can cause/worsen HF: NSAIDs, corticosteroids, estrogens, TZDs (rosi-/pioglitazone), minoxidil, clonidine, excess β-blocker/CCB, class Ia/Ic antiarrhythmics (p1173).":["u20"],"clinical-features-amp-diagnosis|Clinical Features & Diagnosis":["u21"],"copy|Classic signs: pulmonary rales, elevated JVP, abdominojugular reflux, S3 gallop, pitting edema — but often unreliable in older adults (p1173).":["u22"],"copy|Highly specific in older adults: pulsus alternans, S3 gallop, JVD at rest or with abdominojugular reflux maneuver (p1173).":["u23"],"copy|BNP >500 pg/mL in the right clinical context is highly suggestive of active HF; a normal value (<100 pg/mL) in a nonobese older adult makes HF much less likely — but natriuretic-peptide specificity declines with age, and levels are generally lower with higher BMI (p1174).":["u24"],"copy|Diagnostic workup Class I (most patients): CBC, chemistries, TSH, BNP/NT-proBNP, UA, CXR, ECG, 2D echo with Doppler, and cardiac catheterization/coronary angiography if angina or significant ischemia (unless not a revascularization candidate) (Table 76-6, p1174).":["u25"],"copy|HFREF vs HFPEF exam clues: HFREF trends younger/male with prior MI, displaced PMI, S3 gallop, Q waves; HFPEF trends older/female with hypertension/diabetes/obesity/aortic stenosis, preserved PMI, S4 gallop (Table 76-7, p1175).":["u26"],"hfref-vs-hfpef|HFREF vs HFPEF":["u27"],"copy|HFREF \"usually defined as ejection fraction less than 40% to 50%\" (p1175) — the chapter then narrows this to a specific working cutoff for the rest of the discussion: EF <45% for HFREF and EF ≥45% for HFPEF (p1175). Know both numbers (see Exam traps below).":["u28"],"copy|No single clinical feature reliably distinguishes HFREF from HFPEF; echo/MRI/radionuclide angiography needed (p1175).":["u29"],"copy|>50% of older HF patients have HFPEF (p1184).":["u30"],"copy|70–80% of older HFPEF patients have hypertension (p1186).":["u31"],"nonpharmacologic-management|Nonpharmacologic Management":["u32"],"copy|Nonadherence/behavioral factors drive readmission: 64% of exacerbations tied to medication/diet nonadherence in one study, and emotional/environmental factors contributed to 26% of readmissions; in a 140-patient cohort (age ≥70), 47% were readmitted within 90 days (p1176).":["u33"],"copy|Meta-analysis of 33 disease-management trials: HF readmissions ↓42%, all-cause readmissions ↓24%, mortality ↓20% (p1176).":["u34"],"copy|Sodium target 1500–3000 mg/d — expert consensus, not trial-derived (p1176).":["u35"],"copy|Dietary consultation also covers avoidance of excess fluid intake (>2 L/d, especially if Na <130) alongside avoidance of excess sodium (>2–3 g/d), plus low-fat/low-cholesterol and adequate-calorie guidance (Table 76-8, p1177).":["u36"],"copy|Exercise is contraindicated with decompensated NYHA IV HF, severe aortic stenosis/hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, recent MI/unstable angina (within 2wk), life-threatening arrhythmias not adequately treated, or any acute serious illness (eg, pneumonia) (Table 76-9, p1178).":["u37"],"copy|PICNIC trial (120 malnourished patients, mean age 79): intensive dietary counseling → death/HF hospitalization at 1y 27% vs 61% (p<0.001) (p1177).":["u38"],"copy|HF-ACTION (2331 patients, EF ≤35%): exercise → 7% reduction in death/hospitalization, unadjusted (ns, p=0.13); 11% reduction after adjustment (p=0.03) (p1177–1178).":["u39"],"drugs-for-hfref-name-dose-key-caution|Drugs for HFREF (name — dose — key caution)":["u40"],"copy|Carvedilol — start 3.125–6.25 mg BID → target 25–50 mg BID (p1179).":["u41"],"copy|Metoprolol succinate — start 12.5–25 mg daily → target 100–200 mg daily; (tartrate 6.25 mg BID or QID is a titration alternative) (p1179).":["u42"],"copy|Bisoprolol — start 1.25–2.5 mg daily → target 10 mg daily (p1179). The book calls these \"the only three drugs that were studied and subsequently demonstrated benefit\" — yet the same page reports SENIORS (age ≥70, n=2128): nebivolol significantly lowered death or CV hospitalization (p1179).":["u43"],"copy|Captopril — start 6.25–12.5 mg TID; enalapril start 2.5–5 mg BID; monitor BP/renal function/K+ during titration (p1179).":["u44"],"copy|Sacubitril/valsartan (ARNi) — start low, e.g. 24/26 mg PO BID in older adults; needs ≥36h washout from ACEi to avoid angioedema (p1180–1181).":["u45"],"copy|Steroidal MRAs for symptomatic HFrEF: initiate if eGFR >30 mL/min/1.73 m² and serum K <5.0 mEq/L, then monitor both. The chapter lists spironolactone 12.5–50 mg daily and eplerenone 25–50 mg daily and gives an older creatinine cutoff (p1181). DAPA-HF and EMPEROR-Reduced demonstrated improvements in their HF/CV composite outcomes, including in patients without diabetes. Mortality effects differed between trials.Current practice (not the book): eGFR ≤30 mL/min/1.73 m² or serum potassium ≥5.0 mEq/L are contraindications to MRA initiation — AHA/ACC/HFSA heart failure guideline, 2022.":["u46"],"copy|Hydralazine/isosorbide dinitrate — older-adult start 12.5–25 mg TID–QID / 10 mg TID–QID (p1182).":["u47"],"copy|Loop diuretics — furosemide 20–160 mg, bumetanide 0.5–5 mg, torsemide 5–100 mg/day; add metolazone 2.5–10 mg daily if refractory (p1182).":["u48"],"copy|Digoxin — 0.125 mg daily in older adults with normal renal function; target serum level 0.5–0.9 ng/mL; toxicity can occur even at \"therapeutic\" levels (p1182–1183).":["u49"],"copy|Refractory HF — high-dose oral furosemide 200 mg BID or bumetanide 10 mg daily; or continuous IV furosemide 5–40 mg/h or bumetanide 0.5–1 mg/h (p1189).":["u50"],"copy|Tafamidis (ATTR cardiac amyloidosis) — meglumine 80 mg daily or free-salt 61 mg daily (not per-mg substitutable); ATTR-ACT: mortality 29.5% vs 42.9% placebo; cost ~$225,000/year (p1188).":["u51"],"device-therapy|Device Therapy":["u52"],"copy|ICD: NYHA II–III HFREF, EF ≤35% (primary prevention) or post-arrest VT/VF (secondary); benefit declines with age; patients ≥80y are twice as likely as younger patients to have major implant complications (p1184).":["u53"],"copy|CRT: the strongest standard indication includes LVEF ≤35%, sinus rhythm, LBBB and QRS ≥150 ms, with NYHA II, III or ambulatory IV symptoms despite guideline-directed therapy. In selected older adults, CRT improves symptoms, quality of life and survival. ICD therapy targets arrhythmic sudden death (p1184, AHA/ACC/HFSA 2022).":["u54"],"copy|MitraClip (COAPT trial, n=614, HFREF + moderate-severe secondary MR despite max GDMT): lower HF hospitalization and lower all-cause mortality vs medical therapy alone (p1184).":["u55"],"hfpef-specific-therapy|HFPEF-Specific Therapy":["u56"],"copy|SGLT2 inhibitors reduced HF events in preserved/mildly reduced EF trials. Subsequent FINEARTS-HF also demonstrated a reduction with finerenone in the composite rate of total worsening HF events and cardiovascular deaths among participants with LVEF ≥40% (p1184 describes the earlier evidence).":["u57"],"copy|EMPEROR-Preserved: in 5,988 patients with EF >40%, empagliflozin reduced the combined risk of cardiovascular death or hospitalization for HF (HR 0.79, 95% CI 0.69–0.90, p<0.001), mainly through fewer HF hospitalizations. Results were consistent regardless of diabetes. PRESERVED-HF, a smaller dapagliflozin trial of 324 patients, assessed symptoms and exercise function at 12 weeks (p1185, 1188).":["u58"],"copy|Digoxin not recommended in HFPEF except as an adjunct for rate control in AF (p1188).":["u59"],"prognosis-amp-end-of-life|Prognosis & End-of-Life":["u60"],"copy|5-year survival among older adults with established HF is <50%; in patients ≥80y hospitalized for HF, fewer than 25% survive >5 years (p1190).":["u61"],"copy|Death causes: 40–50% progressive HF, 40% arrhythmias, 10–20% other (p1190).":["u62"],"copy|Overall HF survival is lower than for most cancers (p1190).":["u63"],"prevention|Prevention":["u64"],"copy|Hypertension treatment reduces HF incidence by >50% across multiple trials (p1176).":["u65"],"copy|HYVET: treating HTN in patients ≥80y → 64% reduction in incident HF. SPRINT intensive arm (<120 mmHg) → 36% lower rate of acute decompensated HF vs standard (140 mmHg) arm (p1191).":["u66"],"exam-traps-book-contradictions|Exam traps / book contradictions":["u67"],"copy|Two EF numbers for HFREF, not a contradiction — a general range narrowed to a working cutoff: the chapter first gives the general literature range (\"less than 40% to 50%\"), then explicitly adopts EF <45% as its working definition for HFREF (≥45% for HFPEF) for the rest of the discussion (p1175). A question could test either number.":["u68"],"copy|The three guideline-recommended HFrEF beta blockers are carvedilol, metoprolol succinate and bisoprolol. US labeling for bisoprolol alone is for hypertension, although its HFrEF efficacy is established. Among 2,128 patients aged ≥70, SENIORS found a benefit from nebivolol on the composite of death or cardiovascular hospitalization. These are separate evidence and regulatory questions (p1179).":["u69"],"copy|Digoxin lowers HF hospitalizations but has no mortality benefit in either HFREF (DIG trial) or HFPEF (DIG ancillary) — a trap for assuming any drug that reduces hospitalizations also reduces mortality (p1182, p1188).":["u70"],"recall|Recall":["u71"],"copy|CHAPTER 76s / HAZZARD":["u72"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 76 — Heart Failure; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u73"],"copy|1.":["u74"],"copy|What US heart failure prevalence did Hazzard report, and how many affected adults did the cited projection anticipate by 2030?":["u75"],"copy|Hazzard reported about 6.5 million US patients and projected over 8 million by 2030 (p1166).":["u76"],"copy|Both figures refer to the chapter's reporting period and forecast.":["u76"],"copy|2.":["u77"],"copy|Of 2014 primary-HF hospital admissions, what fraction occurred in patients 75 years or older, and what fraction in the (much smaller) population aged 85 and up?":["u78"],"copy|53% were in patients ≥75y; 25% occurred in the 2% of the population aged ≥85y (p1166).":["u79"],"copy|3.":["u80"],"copy|Name the four principal age-related changes in the cardiovascular system that predispose to heart failure.":["u81"],"copy|(1) Reduced β-adrenergic responsiveness (receptor desensitization), (2) increased arterial stiffness/afterload, (3) altered left ventricular diastolic filling with increased reliance on atrial contraction, (4) altered mitochondrial energy metabolism under stress (p1166–1170).":["u82"],"copy|7.":["u83"],"copy|What BNP cutoffs make heart failure highly likely versus much less likely in a nonobese older adult, and why does BNP become a less reliable rule-in test with age?":["u84"],"copy|500 pg/mL is highly suggestive of active HF; <100 pg/mL makes HF much less likely.":["u85"],"copy|Natriuretic peptide levels rise modestly with normal aging (especially in women) and with declining renal function, so specificity for HF declines with age (p1174).":["u86"],"copy|8.":["u87"],"copy|The chapter gives a general EF range for HFREF, then a narrower working cutoff.":["u88"],"copy|What are the two numbers?":["u89"],"copy|General range: \"less than 40% to 50%.\"":["u90"],"copy|Working cutoff adopted for the rest of the chapter's discussion: EF <45% for HFREF (HFPEF is ≥45%) (p1175).":["u91"],"copy|9.":["u92"],"copy|Which three β-blockers are proven, evidence-based agents for HFREF, and what are their target maintenance doses?":["u93"],"copy|Carvedilol 25–50 mg BID, metoprolol succinate 100–200 mg daily, bisoprolol 10 mg daily (p1179).":["u94"],"copy|11.":["u95"],"copy|What renal-function and potassium thresholds govern MRA initiation in HFrEF, and which spironolactone dose range does the chapter describe?":["u96"],"copy|For MRA initiation in HFrEF, eGFR must be above 30 mL/min/1.73 m² and potassium below 5.0 mEq/L.":["u97"],"copy|Monitor both after starting.":["u97"],"copy|The chapter describes spironolactone 12.5–50 mg once daily (p1181, AHA/ACC/HFSA 2022).":["u97"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): eGFR ≤30 mL/min/1.73 m² or serum potassium ≥5.0 mEq/L are contraindications to MRA initiation — AHA/ACC/HFSA heart failure guideline, 2022.":["u98"],"copy|12.":["u99"],"copy|What target serum digoxin concentration is associated with improved survival in HFREF, and does digoxin reduce mortality?":["u100"],"copy|0.5–0.9 ng/mL (DIG analyses: low-dose levels in this range may be associated with improved survival); no — digoxin reduced HF hospitalizations in the DIG trial but did not reduce mortality (34.8% vs 35.1%, not different from placebo) (p1182–1183).":["u101"],"copy|13.":["u102"],"copy|At what ejection fraction and QRS duration is cardiac resynchronization therapy (CRT) indicated, and how does its benefit differ from an ICD's?":["u103"],"copy|The clearest CRT indication is symptomatic NYHA II, III or ambulatory IV HFrEF despite guideline-directed treatment, EF ≤35%, sinus rhythm, LBBB and QRS ≥150 ms.":["u104"],"copy|CRT can improve function and survival in selected octogenarians.":["u105"],"copy|An ICD prevents arrhythmic sudden death (p1184).":["u105"],"copy|14.":["u106"],"copy|Which drug class was tested in EMPEROR-Preserved, and which outcome drove the trial's composite benefit?":["u107"],"copy|Empagliflozin, an SGLT2 inhibitor, reduced cardiovascular death or HF hospitalization in EMPEROR-Preserved (n=5,988, EF >40%, HR 0.79, 95% CI 0.69–0.90).":["u108"],"copy|Fewer HF hospitalizations drove the result, irrespective of diabetes status (p1188).":["u109"],"copy|15.":["u110"],"copy|What is the 5-year survival for older adults with established heart failure, and for patients 80 years or older specifically hospitalized with heart failure?":["u111"],"copy|Less than 50% for older adults generally; fewer than 25% for patients ≥80y hospitalized with heart failure survive more than 5 years (p1190).":["u112"],"copy|Answer":["u113","u114","u115","u116","u117","u118","u119","u120","u121","u122","u123"],"what-the-exams-asked|What the exams asked":["u124"],"copy|Recognize an apical S4 in a hypertensive patient with orthopnea and distinguish impaired relaxation from weak contraction — 2020 Q68, May 2024 Q56.":["u125"],"copy|Identify why a BNP elevation is less specific in an older patient with impaired renal function — June 2023 Q88.":["u126"],"copy|Find the most common precipitant of recurrent congestion and recognize pulmonary edema after IV fluids — June 2023 Q37, June 2023 Basic Q110.":["u127"],"copy|Identify thiamine deficiency as a cause of high-output failure and consider replacement in a patient at risk from poor intake and diuresis — September 2024 Q49, June 2022 Q80.":["u128"],"copy|Recognize carpal tunnel syndrome as a clue to cardiac amyloidosis — June 2022 Q55.":["u129"],"copy|Distinguish upper gastrointestinal protection from the renal and cardiac risks of NSAIDs — May 2024 Q14.":["u130"],"copy|Separate the effect of influenza vaccination from the outcome evidence for beta blockers, SGLT2 inhibitors and loop diuretics — June 2023 Q74. The vaccination paragraph is additional teaching on p1176.":["u131"],"copy|Identify the beta blockers with HFrEF outcome evidence and choose appropriate therapy after pulmonary edema — June 2023 Q96, June 2022 Q66.":["u132"],"copy|Compare sacubitril/valsartan with enalapril and select the hydralazine–nitrate alternative for intolerance — September 2024 Q85, September 2024 Q60.":["u133"],"copy|Distinguish eplerenone from drugs that slow the heart, and interpret digoxin exposure in an older patient with CKD — June 2025 Basic Q149, December 2021 Q5.":["u134"],"copy|Interpret the HFpEF trial endpoints for digoxin, candesartan and empagliflozin — June 2022 Q1, May 2024 Q45. The EMPEROR-Preserved paragraph gives the trial result beyond the printed citation.":["u135"],"copy|Distinguish SGLT2 adverse effects from hypomagnesemia — June 2026 Basic Q147.":["u136"],"copy|Choose CRT for electrical dyssynchrony and consider an LVAD for refractory systolic failure — May 2024 Q51, 2020 Q72, June 2025 Q92.":["u137"],"copy|Identify a favorable prognostic association after HF hospitalization — December 2021 Q59.":["u138"],"reading-the-examination-and-the-bnp-together|Reading the examination and the BNP together":["u139"],"finding-the-precipitant-and-the-underlying-disease|Finding the precipitant and the underlying disease":["u140"],"choosing-treatment-by-the-outcome-it-changes|Choosing treatment by the outcome it changes":["u141"],"hfpef-trials-and-adverse-effects|HFpEF trials and adverse effects":["u142"],"devices-prognosis-and-the-treatment-decision|Devices, prognosis and the treatment decision":["u143"],"israeli-context|Israeli context — שיקום לב בישראל":["u144"],"right-heart-failure-and-care-across-the-course-of-illness|Right heart failure and care across the course of illness":["u145"],"copy|When a patient wakes breathless or coughs up frothy sputum, the first question is whether left-sided filling pressure has risen.":["u146"],"copy|A fourth heart sound just before S1 reflects late filling against a stiff ventricle.":["u147"],"copy|A hypertensive patient with an S4 can develop pulmonary edema without raised neck veins or swollen legs.":["u148"],"copy|In contrast, a displaced apex and an S3 favor a dilated, poorly contracting ventricle.":["u149"],"copy|These findings shift probability rather than measure EF.":["u150"],"copy|An echocardiogram assesses ventricular size, EF, wall thickness, filling and valves (pp1173–1175, Table 76-7).":["u151"],"copy|The aging ventricle relaxes more slowly and relies more on atrial contraction.":["u152"],"copy|Rapid AF shortens diastole and removes the atrial contribution.":["u153"],"copy|A small change in rate or volume can therefore precipitate symptoms even when the EF was preserved at the patient's last examination.":["u154"],"copy|Reduced beta-adrenergic reserve limits the compensatory heart-rate response, and arterial stiffness increases the load against which the ventricle ejects (pp1167–1170).":["u155"],"copy|BNP reflects myocardial stretch.":["u156"],"copy|An older person with CKD can have a high result without a new episode of pulmonary edema.":["u157"],"copy|Age, particularly in women, and anemia also push levels upward, while higher BMI pushes them downward.":["u158"],"copy|In a nonobese older patient a value below 100 pg/mL weighs against active HF, whereas a value above 500 pg/mL supports it when the history and examination agree.":["u159"],"copy|Neither value distinguishes HFrEF from HFpEF.":["u160"],"copy|Consider alternative pulmonary causes and use chest imaging, ECG and Doppler echocardiography to answer the next question (pp1173–1175).":["u161"],"copy|In established HF, ask what changed before escalating the maintenance dose.":["u162"],"copy|Missing tablets, being unable to obtain refills, urinary urgency after the morning diuretic, or dependence on salty prepared food can all produce recurrent congestion.":["u163"],"copy|In the series summarized in the chapter, difficulty following medication or diet accounted for up to two-thirds of exacerbations.":["u164"],"copy|Among cardiac triggers, ischemia and atrial arrhythmias are especially important.":["u165"],"copy|Infection, anemia, thyroid disease, acute kidney dysfunction and newly prescribed drugs can further narrow an older patient's limited reserve (pp1172–1173, Table 76-4).":["u166"],"copy|In the June 2023 Basic case a woman with diastolic HF becomes hypoxemic soon after receiving IV fluid for a febrile urinary infection.":["u167"],"copy|The bilateral opacities, timing and limited capacity to accommodate volume favor hydrostatic edema.":["u168"],"copy|Support oxygenation, reassess further fluids, treat the congestion and continue treating the infection.":["u169"],"copy|The printed range begins with the precipitant on p1172.":["u170"],"copy|The diuretic paragraph explains the treatment separately (pp1172–1175, 1182).":["u171"],"copy|With systemic NSAIDs, reduced prostaglandin activity can both damage the upper gastrointestinal mucosa and impair renal sodium excretion.":["u172"],"copy|A proton-pump inhibitor lowers the likelihood of upper gastrointestinal ulcer bleeding but does not prevent kidney injury, higher pressure or congestion.":["u173"],"copy|Review over-the-counter analgesics in someone whose diuretics appear to have stopped working.":["u174"],"copy|May 2024 Q14 draws the gastrointestinal teaching from Chapter 52 and the HF exacerbation from Chapter 76 (pp787–788, 1173).":["u175"],"copy|Thiamine deficiency, severe chronic anemia, hyperthyroidism and arteriovenous shunting can cause high-output failure.":["u176"],"copy|A high cardiac output still falls short of the body's requirements.":["u177"],"copy|Wet beriberi is the classic exam association.":["u178"],"copy|Look for limited food intake or prolonged diuretic exposure when deficiency is plausible.":["u179"],"copy|Replace deficiency without assuming that all patients taking furosemide benefit from routine supplementation (pp1171–1172, Table 76-3).":["u180"],"copy|Amyloid infiltration produces stiff, thickened ventricles.":["u181"],"copy|Prior carpal tunnel syndrome, especially bilateral disease, is a clue when HF coincides with otherwise unexplained thickening or atrial arrhythmia.":["u182"],"copy|An EF of 40% has many possible causes, and normal BNP does not identify cardiac amyloid.":["u183"],"copy|All four options received credit in June 2022 Q55.":["u184"],"copy|In a selected group of older patients hospitalized with HFpEF and LV wall thickness over 12 mm, 13% had wild-type ATTR cardiac amyloidosis.":["u185"],"copy|That denominator matters when considering testing (p1171, ACC cardiac amyloidosis consensus 2023, Table 1).":["u186"],"copy|For stable HFrEF, distinguish the four disease-modifying treatment classes from diuretics for symptom relief.":["u187"],"copy|An ARNI or suitable alternative modifies renin–angiotensin signaling, evidence-based beta blockade reduces harmful sympathetic stimulation, an MRA blocks aldosterone and an SGLT2 inhibitor lowers HF events even without diabetes.":["u188"],"copy|Start and titrate around blood pressure, pulse, kidney function, potassium and what matters to the patient.":["u189"],"copy|With persistent edema, a small increase in creatinine alone is insufficient reason to stop decongestion.":["u190"],"copy|If the patient instead becomes dry and orthostatic, reduce the diuretic intensity and reassess (pp1179–1183).":["u191"],"copy|Carvedilol, sustained-release metoprolol succinate and bisoprolol have HFrEF outcome evidence.":["u192"],"copy|Atenolol and propranolol cannot inherit those outcomes simply by belonging to the same class.":["u193"],"copy|SENIORS also found that nebivolol reduced death or cardiovascular hospitalization in a population aged 70 or older.":["u194"],"copy|These findings support starting an appropriate drug after stabilization.":["u195"],"copy|Severe decompensation, marked bradycardia and advanced heart block change the immediate decision (p1179).":["u196"],"copy|Compared with enalapril in PARADIGM-HF, sacubitril/valsartan reduced cardiovascular death or HF hospitalization, with more hypotension but less renal impairment and severe hyperkalemia.":["u197"],"copy|The risk is lower, not absent.":["u198"],"copy|Check pressure, creatinine and potassium and allow at least 36 hours after an ACE inhibitor to reduce angioedema risk.":["u199"],"copy|If first-line renin–angiotensin agents cannot be tolerated, hydralazine plus isosorbide dinitrate offers combined arterial and venous dilation.":["u200"],"copy|The evidence is for the combination, and multiple daily doses matter when planning support at home (pp1180–1182).":["u201"],"copy|Eplerenone does not directly slow the heart.":["u202"],"copy|Bisoprolol acts through beta blockade, ivabradine slows the sinus pacemaker, and digoxin slows AV nodal conduction.":["u203"],"copy|In a euvolemic older patient with controlled AF, CKD and a digoxin level of 1.4 ng/mL, reconsider the dose before attributing exertional symptoms to inadequate diuresis.":["u204"],"copy|The chapter's low HF concentration range is 0.5–0.9 ng/mL.":["u205"],"copy|Declining renal function and hypokalemia increase toxicity risk even when a laboratory labels the concentration therapeutic (pp1179, 1181–1183).":["u206"],"copy|The June 2022 post-discharge question credits two options.":["u207"],"copy|For someone taking propranolol, a switch to carvedilol after stabilization addresses HFrEF outcomes.":["u208"],"copy|Adding spironolactone can also be appropriate if eGFR is above 30 mL/min/1.73 m² and potassium is below 5.0 mEq/L, with both rechecked after starting.":["u209"],"copy|The fluid advice bundled with each option must be considered separately.":["u210"],"copy|Table 76-8 warns against excess intake, especially with hyponatremia.":["u211"],"copy|Four cups per day is not a general HF prescription, and avoiding all exertion can accelerate deconditioning (pp1176–1179, 1181, AHA/ACC/HFSA 2022, §7.3.3).":["u212"],"copy|Influenza can trigger decompensation, so vaccination belongs in the management plan.":["u213"],"copy|The chapter describes an association with lower mortality.":["u214"],"copy|The subsequent randomized IVVE trial found a mortality reduction during peak influenza circulation but no significant mortality dif